WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.833
Picture a small theatre company putting on a play.

2
00:00:05.833 --> 00:00:09.180
A new prop, say a crown, doesn't just appear on stage.

3
00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:10.357
Someone fetches it.

4
00:00:10.357 --> 00:00:14.572
The crew checks it, and gives it a labelled spot on the props table.

5
00:00:14.572 --> 00:00:18.105
And only at the dress rehearsal does an actor pick it up.

6
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:21.786
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

7
00:00:21.786 --> 00:00:26.450
When my code mentions a class, its start-up code runs right then, doesn't it?

8
00:00:26.450 --> 00:00:28.449
Mention a class, and it wakes up?

9
00:00:29.533 --> 00:00:31.616
Let's start from code you know.

10
00:00:31.616 --> 00:00:33.699
A class can have start-up code.

11
00:00:33.699 --> 00:00:37.865
In Java, that's a static block, and static fields with values.

12
00:00:37.865 --> 00:00:40.552
In Kotlin, an object with an init block.

13
00:00:40.552 --> 00:00:45.524
The JVM, the program that runs your compiled code, runs it once per class.

14
00:00:45.667 --> 00:00:47.792
Here's a tiny class, Limits.

15
00:00:47.792 --> 00:00:50.448
MAX_CAST is a static final int, 12.

16
00:00:50.448 --> 00:00:53.408
CAP is a static final Integer, also 12.

17
00:00:53.408 --> 00:00:55.912
And the static block prints init.

18
00:00:56.067 --> 00:01:00.442
Now another class prints Limits.MAX_CAST, and then Limits.CAP.

19
00:01:00.442 --> 00:01:01.995
When does init appear?

20
00:01:01.995 --> 00:01:05.524
Before the first value, between the two, or never?

21
00:01:05.524 --> 00:01:06.653
Pause and guess.

22
00:01:09.867 --> 00:01:11.409
Hold on to that guess.

23
00:01:11.409 --> 00:01:13.932
To check it, we need one fact first.

24
00:01:13.932 --> 00:01:16.876
Bringing in a class sounds like one event.

25
00:01:16.876 --> 00:01:18.768
Inside the JVM, it's three.

26
00:01:19.333 --> 00:01:20.762
Back to the theatre.

27
00:01:20.762 --> 00:01:23.333
A new prop goes through three steps.

28
00:01:23.333 --> 00:01:25.189
First, someone fetches it.

29
00:01:25.189 --> 00:01:26.261
That's loading.

30
00:01:26.261 --> 00:01:35.473
The JVM finds the class file's bytes, in a folder or a jar, and turns them into a Class object, with a method called defineClass.

31
00:01:35.667 --> 00:01:39.065
Second, before any scene uses it, the crew gets it ready.

32
00:01:39.065 --> 00:01:40.317
They check it's safe.

33
00:01:40.317 --> 00:01:43.656
They give it an empty, labelled spot on the props table.

34
00:01:43.656 --> 00:01:47.174
And they fill in the cue sheet that says where it's needed.

35
00:01:47.174 --> 00:01:50.036
That's linking, and it has the same three parts.

36
00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:51.937
Checking is verification.

37
00:01:51.937 --> 00:01:59.997
The JVM checks that the bytecode, the instructions inside, is type-safe: no instruction treats a number as a string.

38
00:01:59.997 --> 00:02:02.081
The empty spot is preparation.

39
00:02:02.081 --> 00:02:07.501
The static fields are created, and set to their defaults: zero, false or null.

40
00:02:07.501 --> 00:02:09.724
And the cue sheet is resolution.

41
00:02:09.724 --> 00:02:13.963
Names, like method fire in class Lights, become direct links.

42
00:02:14.133 --> 00:02:19.253
Third comes the dress rehearsal, the only step that puts actors on stage.

43
00:02:19.253 --> 00:02:20.796
That's initialization.

44
00:02:20.796 --> 00:02:25.073
The JVM runs the class's static start-up code, top to bottom.

45
00:02:25.073 --> 00:02:32.016
javac, the Java compiler, collects all of it into one method, named &lt;clinit&gt;, short for class init.

46
00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:34.600
So two of the three steps are crew work.

47
00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:38.799
Nobody claps for the crew, and none of your code runs while they work.

48
00:02:38.799 --> 00:02:40.839
Only the rehearsal runs your code.

49
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:42.481
So the point is this.

50
00:02:42.481 --> 00:02:47.275
Loading a class, and running its start-up code, are separate events.

51
00:02:47.275 --> 00:02:49.108
They can happen far apart.

52
00:02:49.108 --> 00:02:54.467
HotSpot, the JVM inside your JDK, runs each step as late as it's allowed to.

53
00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:58.244
And here's where the theatre picture breaks.

54
00:02:58.244 --> 00:03:01.476
A real crew checks the whole prop before the show.

55
00:03:01.476 --> 00:03:08.199
The JVM may fill in the cue sheet lazily, one name at a time, and never at all for code that never runs.

56
00:03:08.333 --> 00:03:09.709
The lesson tried it.

57
00:03:09.709 --> 00:03:13.904
A class that used a deleted Helper class loaded and ran fine.

58
00:03:13.904 --> 00:03:19.269
It failed with NoClassDefFoundError only when the branch that used Helper ran.

59
00:03:19.269 --> 00:03:23.464
And a class with broken bytecode was accepted by defineClass.

60
00:03:23.464 --> 00:03:27.865
The VerifyError came only when code first asked for its methods.

61
00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:30.907
So which uses start the rehearsal?

62
00:03:30.907 --> 00:03:34.367
The Java Language Specification lists them exactly.

63
00:03:34.367 --> 00:03:36.266
Creating an object with new.

64
00:03:36.266 --> 00:03:37.895
Calling a static method.

65
00:03:37.895 --> 00:03:39.591
Assigning a static field.

66
00:03:39.591 --> 00:03:42.644
Reading a static field that isn't a constant.

67
00:03:42.644 --> 00:03:45.765
Reflection, such as Class.forName with a name.

68
00:03:45.765 --> 00:03:49.496
And a subclass always initializes its superclass first.

69
00:03:49.667 --> 00:03:50.858
And these don't.

70
00:03:50.858 --> 00:03:52.272
Reading a constant.

71
00:03:52.272 --> 00:03:53.836
Writing Limits.class.

72
00:03:53.836 --> 00:03:56.218
Making an empty array of Limits.

73
00:03:56.218 --> 00:04:00.089
Or Class.forName, with false as its second argument.

74
00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:02.554
Now, back to your guess.

75
00:04:02.554 --> 00:04:08.704
The lesson's InitPuzzle runs eight steps, and prints an indented line whenever a &lt;clinit&gt; runs.

76
00:04:08.704 --> 00:04:10.193
Here's its real output.

77
00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:14.187
Step 3 reads Limits.MAX_CAST, and nothing prints.

78
00:04:14.187 --> 00:04:18.432
Step 4 reads Limits.CAP, and there it is: Limits.&lt;clinit&gt;.

79
00:04:18.432 --> 00:04:21.287
So init appears between the two values.

80
00:04:21.467 --> 00:04:23.427
The rest of the list agrees.

81
00:04:23.427 --> 00:04:30.498
Step 1 reads parentValue through Child, and only Parent wakes up, because Parent declares that field.

82
00:04:30.498 --> 00:04:32.038
new Child wakes Child.

83
00:04:32.038 --> 00:04:36.239
The array, Lazy.class, and forName with false print nothing.

84
00:04:36.239 --> 00:04:39.249
Only Lazy.touch, a static call, wakes Lazy.

85
00:04:40.133 --> 00:04:42.042
So why is MAX_CAST different?

86
00:04:42.042 --> 00:04:48.558
It's a constant variable: a static final primitive or String whose value the compiler can work out.

87
00:04:48.558 --> 00:04:52.375
javac copied the 12 straight into the caller's class file.

88
00:04:52.375 --> 00:04:55.797
At run time, the caller never touches Limits at all.

89
00:04:55.933 --> 00:04:57.279
CAP is an Integer.

90
00:04:57.279 --> 00:05:01.016
To store 12 in it, the code calls Integer.valueOf.

91
00:05:01.016 --> 00:05:05.575
A method call isn't a constant, so reading CAP is a real use.

92
00:05:06.467 --> 00:05:11.274
A constant is like a line the director photocopied into everyone's script.

93
00:05:11.274 --> 00:05:14.522
Nobody phones the props table to find out it's 12.

94
00:05:14.522 --> 00:05:16.471
They just read their own copy.

95
00:05:16.667 --> 00:05:20.570
So reading a constant isn't a use of its class at all.

96
00:05:20.570 --> 00:05:21.437
It's a copy.

97
00:05:22.333 --> 00:05:24.173
Kotlin draws the same line.

98
00:05:24.173 --> 00:05:27.035
const val MAX_CAST is copied into callers.

99
00:05:27.035 --> 00:05:34.599
A plain val CAP becomes a private field and a getter on the object, so reading it runs the object's init block.

100
00:05:35.467 --> 00:05:38.468
Now the names, for things you've already seen.

101
00:05:38.468 --> 00:05:40.165
Loading fetches the bytes.

102
00:05:40.165 --> 00:05:42.775
Linking verifies, prepares and resolves.

103
00:05:42.775 --> 00:05:46.821
And initialization runs &lt;clinit&gt;, once, on the first real use.

104
00:05:47.733 --> 00:05:49.505
Three rules to take with you.

105
00:05:49.505 --> 00:05:51.948
One: mentioning a class is not using it.

106
00:05:51.948 --> 00:05:56.713
If you need its start-up code to have run, check that your use is on the list.

107
00:05:56.867 --> 00:06:04.195
Two: turn a constant into a computed value, like a number read from a setting, and you change when the class starts up.

108
00:06:04.195 --> 00:06:06.843
That's the day the rehearsal quietly moves.

109
00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:15.156
Three: a missing class shows up as NoClassDefFoundError when the code that needs it first runs, not when the program starts.

110
00:06:16.067 --> 00:06:18.698
So, does mentioning a class wake it up?

111
00:06:18.698 --> 00:06:18.900
No.

112
00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:21.802
Fetch, then check and label, then rehearse.

113
00:06:21.802 --> 00:06:26.120
Only the rehearsal runs your code, and only a real use calls it.

114
00:06:26.267 --> 00:06:27.322
Quick check.

115
00:06:27.322 --> 00:06:29.872
Some code runs new Limits[3].

116
00:06:29.872 --> 00:06:31.279
Does init print?

117
00:06:34.333 --> 00:06:34.568
No.

118
00:06:34.568 --> 00:06:39.187
An empty array holds no Limits yet, so the rehearsal waits.

119
00:06:39.187 --> 00:06:40.988
Step 5 printed nothing.
